From 34ebc2ec7344938d23ca2f83684776a213645ad8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 18:17:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dvbridge: send the Dolby Vision metadata as CCM 001 and
 dovi_tool describe it

An audit of the metadata serializer against ETSI GS CCM 001 V1.1.1 and
dovi_tool found its header, block format, packets, CRC, parity and matrices
right, and these places where real content lost rows of its picture or its
native output, or the display was told something false:

- Level 5 of a cropped or scaled encode. The offsets describe the canvas
  that the RPU was made for. An encode that cropped the bars off (3840x1600)
  or scaled the master down (1920x1080, 1920x800) and kept its RPU still has
  offsets of the 3840x2160 master. Read as offsets of the decoded picture,
  they painted real rows black: 280 at each end of a 3840x1600 crop placed
  at row 280, and the FEL test stream itself, decoded at 1920x1080, lost 275
  rows at the top and 276 at the bottom. Nothing in the metadata says which
  canvas it is, so the offsets are also read as those of a master that the
  picture fills in width or in height, lowered by the part that was cropped
  on that axis (by all of it on each side, as where it was cropped is not
  known: never less than the bars really lost), and each side takes the
  smaller margin of the two readings. A wrong reading can only leave a part
  of the bars unpainted (they are black in the picture all the same, and the
  display is told of smaller bars); it never paints the picture. A 3840x2160
  picture is read as before. Offsets that fit only the master (a 1280x536
  encode) no longer refuse the frame. Logged once per stream.
- A payload of up to 512 bytes (CCM 001 6.3.3.3). The packets went to the
  shaders one byte to a 32-bit word, so the 128 uvec4 uniforms held four
  packets and the serializer refused more than 482 bytes. Four bytes to a
  word, the first in the high bits, hold 16 packets in the same uniforms;
  the payload may now take its 512 bytes (five packets). The direct way
  uploaded the packet words twice when it wrote the bars first (both hooks
  are in one shader): they are declared once. Streams that passed before give
  the same bytes.
- A frame that failed or was not presented reset the serializer: the
  metadata id went back to 0 and the next frame carried scene_refresh_flag
  = 1, a scene cut of its own making, after every packed render that fell
  back and every frame that was not shown. Such a frame never changed the
  state (it is committed only when presented), so the renderer now only
  recovers libplacebo there (dvbridge_renderer_recover()). A new stream
  (dvbridge_reset()) still refreshes the scene, and its metadata id goes on
  from the last one instead of starting at 0 again, so the display never sees
  an id that it may still hold metadata of.
- Level 11. dovi_tool reads the whitepoint from bits 0-3 of byte 1 and the
  reference mode flag from bit 4; FFmpeg reads the whitepoint from bits 4-7.
  The check refused bits 0-2 and any byte 2: every frame of a title with a
  whitepoint of 1 to 7 was refused, and the title lost native output. It now
  checks what dovi_tool validates (content type at most 15, byte 3 zero); the
  bytes still go to the display as they are.
- Extension blocks of a level that CCM 001 reserves refused the frame; a
  display ignores them. They are left out, and logged once.
- A scene cut is lost with a frame that is not shown. The next frame was
  made to refresh the scene only after a gap of 100 ms, and one frame missing
  at 24 frames a second is 83 ms. A gap of more than 1.5 frame periods now
  refreshes. The period comes from the player (VideoPicture::iDuration,
  through the picture buffer of the renderer). It is not used while more than
  half of the last 16 gaps, the current one among them, are longer (a frame
  rate that does not describe the stream, or frames dropped all the time),
  and the 100 ms rule holds as well, so a refresh that it gave is never taken
  away.
- An RPU without extension blocks could not be read at all
  (dvbridge_dv_bounds() demanded one), so it could not be reconstructed for
  output as HDR10 either. dvbridge_dv_readable() checks the regions of the
  metadata and the blocks it has; native output still needs the display
  management blocks (the serializer requires level 1).
- PROVENANCE.json gave the hashes of the two headers as they were
  integrated, which the files no longer have: they are kept as
  upstream_files_sha256, with a note.

Tested on the host: a serializer test (59 checks: margins, packets read back
byte by byte, prepare/commit/reset sequences, gaps, level 11, reserved
levels, readable metadata) fails 35 of them with the previous bridge and
passes with this one, and the render test on a software OpenGL ES draws the
FEL test stream with byte-identical output in its 36 full-raster drawings.

On top of the overview page of the player information ("dvbridge: keep
what the display is sent of each frame's metadata", "estuary: an overview
page in the player information"): blocks of reserved levels are left out
of the payload that the page reads back, so they show in its "Not sent"
row next to L6 and L255, and the comment of that row says so.
